+# FAA recording order and equipment inventory leads
+
+**November 9, 2026 | B129 / PD03–PD04.** FAA Order 1600.24D identifies two annual recording and monitoring equipment filings and a written approval trail. These are identifiable records to seek for the FAA's Washington Operations Center Complex (WOCC). The May 2001 FAA checklist lists the order after removal of the federal regulatory chapter it cites. The policy documents establish what their authors specified. The inventory returns, line-specific approvals, recordings and case-specific recommendation outcomes remain unacquired.
+
+## The 1995 FAA records trail
+
+| Observation | Exact locator |
+| --- | --- |
+| The order face dates issuance to `10/10/95`. It cites 41 CFR § 201–21.603 and DOT Order 1600.17B dated September 21, 1990. It cancels FAA Order 1600.24C and the prior annual report identified on the face as `RIS:SE-1600-26`. The text layer gives `R1S:SE-1600-26`. | Physical p. 1, text lines 4–13, 17–21 and page image. [FAA order, p. 1](https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/order/1600.24d.pdf#page=1) |
+| Paragraph 13 calls for prior documentation of the determination approving covered public-safety recording or monitoring. It lists an official public notice, manual or order among possible forms and cites command centers and air traffic control as examples. Paragraph 14 assigns agency-wide oversight and equipment records. In § 14(b) the page image reads `ACO` while the text layer gives `ACQ`. | Physical p. 3, lines 17–38 and image of § 14(b). [FAA order, p. 3](https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/order/1600.24d.pdf#page=3) |
+| Paragraph 14 identifies annual regional and center listings to **ACO-400 by July 7** and an FAA-wide consolidated statement to **M-70 by July 15**. It also specifies written determinations and procurement approvals routed through the servicing security element to ACS-1 before monitoring begins, review at least every two years, and device records that include quantity, cost, justification and location. Appendix 2 supplies the listing format and a sample transmittal. These listed filings are distinct from the earlier annual report named in the cancellation paragraph. | Physical p. 4, lines 3–9, 19–22, 26–39. Physical pp. 7–8, lines 4–17 and 21–29, respectively. [FAA order, p. 4](https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/order/1600.24d.pdf#page=4) · [Appendix 2](https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/order/1600.24d.pdf#page=7) |
+| Paragraph 15(c) describes monitoring FAA Operations Centers interagency teleconferences for service quality. It separately describes participant-requested recording with announcement. Paragraph 15(d) permits routine monitoring of emergency calls and provides that the exception ceases to apply if a participant objects. These provisions leave actual line equipment, call handling and recording existence as separate factual questions. | Physical p. 5, lines 26–33 and page image. [FAA order, p. 5](https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/order/1600.24d.pdf#page=5) |
+
+## Federal regulation, checklist and later report
+
+- **Federal rule.** GSA's January 2, 1996 amendment took effect February 1, 1996. It specifies signed determinations for the covered cases and requires files documenting decisions to continue or terminate the program after periodic review. GSA later removed and reserved Chapter 201 effective August 8, 1996. The amendment postdates issuance of the 1995 order. The original pre-amendment rule and FAA transition instructions were outside this comparison. [Amendment, 61 FR 10 and 16, physical pp. 2 and 8, lines 85–86 and 102–139](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1996-01-02/pdf/95-31544.pdf) · [Removal, 61 FR 39359, physical p. 1, lines 84 and 143–149](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1996-07-29/pdf/96-19184.pdf)
+- **FAA checklist.** WA 0000.5E dated `5/31/01` has a current-orders row for `1600.24D *`, with `ACP-300`, issue-date field `10110195`, and `Reval Date` field `10/1/01`. Figure 1 separately prints `10/10/95` for the order. Retain the fields as printed. The date in the revalidation column identifies a record to seek. Completion of the revalidation and its effect remain unverified. [WA 0000.5E, physical p. 18 row and column headers, text lines 1–12, 41–43 and 78–79](https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Order/0000.5E.pdf#page=18) · [Figure 1, physical p. 3, order ID at line 85 and visible row](https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Order/0000.5E.pdf#page=3)
+- **Separate later records.** The 2003 DOT order identifies a filing route to M-40 for implementing directives. The 1995 FAA order identifies the M-70 route for consolidated equipment statements. Office succession and actual filings require their own documentation. DOT OIG's 2006 report says the FAA lacked recording capability for WOCC lines before September 11, later recorded most WOCC lines including the Domestic Events Network, and kept recordings six months in practice while lacking a retention policy for those lines. Recommendation 3 calls for a formal preservation policy. The earlier general order and the OIG's line-specific capability and retention account address different questions. Keep recommendation 4 on procedures and the p. 12 correction/personnel recommendations separately tracked. [2003 DOT order review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/0d6efe78c2a2370d582c65c5acb1b5669a0c9017/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-dot-recording-determinations-and-recertification-2026-11-09/README.md) · [OIG CC-2006-085, p. 11 lines 11–18 and p. 13 lines 2–7](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=11)
